# Systems as Attack Vectors

## Summary
Systems can be exploited by threat actors without the 
user's knowledge through vulnerabilities in software, 
hardware, and supply chains. Keeping systems patched 
and updated is one of the most critical defensive measures.

## Key Concepts
- Vulnerabilities — Security flaws that can be exploited 
  by threat actors
- Supply Chain Attack — If a threat actor breaches one 
  app or library connected to thousands of other apps, 
  all of them become compromised
- Zero-Day — A vulnerability discovered and exploited by 
  an attacker before the vendor or public knows about it
- CVE — Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures. A public 
  library of discovered and documented vulnerabilities
- Patch — The fix released by a vendor in response to a 
  CVE or discovered vulnerability
- Exploit — The actual code or method used to take 
  advantage of a vulnerability
- Attack Surface — The total number of possible entry 
  points an attacker can use to get into a system

## Why Systems Are Vulnerable
- Software is written by humans and humans make mistakes
- Legacy systems that are outdated and no longer supported
- Patches are not always applied quickly enough
- Supply chain dependencies mean one breach affects many
- Zero-days give attackers an advantage before defenders 
  even know a vulnerability exists

## How Attackers Exploit Systems
- 81 percent of breaches involve stolen or weak passwords
- Breaching one app or library and pushing a malicious 
  update to all connected users
- Exploiting zero-day vulnerabilities before a patch exists
- Targeting outdated systems that are no longer receiving 
  security updates
- Using publicly known CVEs against unpatched systems

## How Defenders Protect Against This
- Monitoring the CVE database for newly discovered 
  vulnerabilities
- Applying patches as quickly as possible after release
- Reducing attack surface by removing unnecessary software
- Using vulnerability scanners to find weaknesses before 
  attackers do
- Implementing a patch management policy across the 
  organization

## My Key Takeaway
Systems will always have vulnerabilities because software 
is written by humans. The goal of a defender is not to 
achieve perfect security but to patch and respond faster 
than an attacker can exploit.

## Questions I Still Have
- How do we ensure that security training is actually 
  working
- What are all the ways an organization can be exploited 
  through social engineering
- How quickly are organizations expected to patch after 
  a CVE is published
- What happens when a patch breaks existing systems
